rations on  these data sets so  data frame set operations come with  dplyr you take two data frames and get  the union of rows that appear in both  data frames  the intersection of rows appear in both  data frames  and the set difference of rows that  appear in both data frames  that's just your basic union  intersection set different things  applied to data frames  now normally when you want to put data  sets together you need to do something a  little more involved and that's what i  call joining data  so here's a trivial example here's two  data frames they both talk about the  beatles  one's a list of songs and who wrote each  of the songs then one's a list of the  artists in the beelze and what each one  played or one of the instruments they  played and if you wanted to put these  things together into the same data set  so you match up the instrument that each  beatle played  with you know what song they wrote  you can do that with the left join  what left join does is it takes the  names of two data frames and then it  takes some joining criteria these would  be the name or names or columns that  both data sets share  and then what it will do is it'll  go through the second data frame find  the pieces of information that apply to  rows in the first data frame and add  them where they belong in that data  frame so order is not preserved the  second data frame's order is not  preserved first day frame yes that is  preserved but things are moved around to  supply a play column that actually makes  sense with your data frame and at the  end you have rows in your your output  that describe a unified observation john  both wrote across the universe and  played guitar  guitar had to appear twice  because john is also in the second row  and so it appears twice  there's no information about this last  row buddy holly who made piggy sue so  for the place called necketts and n a  there's nothing we could put there  left joins aren't the only way to join  data together in